<section xml:id="section-ideals">
  <title>Ring Homomorphisms and Ideasl</title>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-commutative-algebra">
    <title>Commutative Algebra</title>
    <p>
      The ring theory of non-commutative rings is a fascinating part
      of abstract algebra. But using non-commutative rings makes many
      of the definitions of ring theory more complciated. Therefore,
      to present a cleaner development, I'm going to assume from now
      on that all rings are commutative. 
    </p>
    <p>
      This means that what we are doing now is <term>commutative
      algebra</term>. Even though the general term <sq>algebra</sq> is
      being used, <sq>commutative algebra</sq> means commutative ring
      theory. Commutative algebra is the basis of many applications of
      ring theory, mostly notably algebraic geometry. Many ring-theory
      textbooks are just called things like <sq>An Introduction to
      Commutative Algebra</sq>, since commutative rings are so central
      to many uses of ring theory. All this to say there is pretty
      good justification for us, in an introductory course, to
      restrict to commutative rings. 
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-ring-homomorphisms">
    <title>Ring Homomorphisms</title>
    <p>
      In the group theory section, I mentioned several times the
      importance of the functions between groups. We understand groups
      by the functions between them. This becomes even more obvious at
      the end of that chapter, where representations where
      homomorphisms that allowed the study of strange and difficult
      groups. Homomorphisms are central. 
    </p>
    <p>
      The same attitude holds throughout abstract algebra, including
      rings. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>R</m> and <m>S</m> be rings. A <term>ring
          homomorphism</term> is a function <m>\phi: R \rightarrow
          S</m> such that these two properties hold for all <m>r_1,
          r_2 \in R</m>. 
          <ul>
            <li>
              <me>\phi(r_1 + r_2) = \phi(r_1) + \phi(r_2)</me>
            </li>
            <li>
              <me>\phi(r_1r_2) = \phi(r_1) \phi(r_2)</me>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      This is very much like a group homomorphism. However, now that a
      ring has two binary operations, a ring homomorphism has to
      cooperate with both operations. To verify that something is a
      ring homomorphism, I would have to check both cooperations. 
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        Let <m>\pi_n: \ZZ \rightarrow \ZZ/n\ZZ</m> be the map that sents
        each integer to its class modulo <m>n</m>. I claim this is a
        ring homomorphism. There is actually very little here to
        check: all of the arithmetic of the integers still works
        modulo <m>n</m>, and it doesn't matter if I reduce modulo
        <m>n</m> before or after multiplication or addition. This
        means that both operations cooperate with the function, so
        it is a ring homomorphism. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Let <m>a \in \RR</m>. Define a map <m>\phi_a: \RR[x]
        \rightarrow \RR</m> which takes any polynomial <m>p(x)</m> and
        evaluates the polymomial at <m>x = a</m>. 
        <me>
          \phi_a(p(x)) = p(a) 
        </me>
        This is called the <term>evaluation map</term> at the real number
        <m>a</m>. I claim that this is an homomorphism. I can check
        both conditions. 
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \phi_a(p(x) + q(x)) = p(a) + q(a) = \phi_a(p(x)) +
            \phi_a(q(x))
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \phi_a(p(x)q(x)) = p(a)q(a) = \phi_a(p(x)) \phi_b(q(x))
          </mrow>
        </md>
        There isn't again much to check here. Evaluation of polyomials
        can happen before or after addition or multiplication, so this
        is a ring homomorphism. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        There is a function <m>\det: M_n(\RR) \rightarrow \RR</m>
        which evaluates the determinant of any matrix. Is this a ring
        homomorphism? From linear algebra, I know it cooperates with
        multiplication.
        <me>
          \det(AB) = \det(A) \det (B) 
        </me>
        However, if does not cooperate with addition. I can see this
        using the identity matrix. If I do the addition first, I get a
        matrix with <m>2</m> on each diagonal entry. The determinant
        of a diagonal matrix is the product of its diagonal entries. 
        <me>
          \det(\Id + \Id) = 2^n
        </me>
        But if I do the determinant first, I get this sum. 
        <me>
          \det(\Id) + \det(\Id) = 1 + 1 = 2 
        </me>
        Assumine <m>n \geq 2</m>, these are not the same. Therefore,
        the determinant is not a ring homomorphism. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      This situation with the determinant is a common one: many
      algebraic operations can fit with one of the two operations, but
      not the other. Therefore, in some sense, ring homomorphisms are a
      bit more restrictive than group homomorphisms. I won't be
      able to make ring homomorphisms out of most group homomorphisms.
      The map <m>\ZZ \rightarrow \ZZ</m> which is multiplication by
      <m>3</m> agrees with addition (due to distribution), but does
      not multiplication. The absolute value function <m>\RR
      \rightarrow \RR</m> agrees with multiplication but not addition.
      I could go on: finding something that actually works with both
      operations is pretty tricky. 
    </p>
    <p>
      That said, ring homomorphisms certainly do exists and certainly
      are important. Let me move of to some properties. 
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\phi: R \rightarrow S</m> be a ring homomorphism.
          Then the following properties are true. 
          <ul>
            <li>
              <m>\phi(0) = 0</m>
            </li>
            <li>
              If both rings are rings with unity, then <m>\phi(1) =
              1</m>. 
            </li>
            <li>
              <m>\phi(-r) = -\phi(r)</m>
            </li>
            <li>
              If both rings are rings with unity and if <m>r</m> is a
              unit, then <m>\phi(r^{-1}) = (\phi(r))^{-1}</m>. 
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      These last two properties mean that a ring homomorphism must be
      a group homomorphism for the unit group (at least when the ring
      has identity and, therefore, the unit group is actually
      defined.) 
    </p>
    <p>
      Several important definitions that we know from group
      homomorphism still hold. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\phi:R \rightarrow S</m> be  ring homomorphism. 
          <ul>
            <li>
              The <term>kernel</term> of <m>\phi</m> is the set of all
              elements of <m>R</m> which are sent to <m>0 \in S</m>.
              (Note the kernel is defined by the <em>additive</em>
              inverse.) The kernel is a subring of <m>R</m>.
            </li>
            <li>
              The <term>image</term> of <m>\phi</m> is the set of all
              output of <m>\phi</m>. The image is a subring of
              <m>S</m>. 
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      The definition of a ring isomorphism is also in parallel with
      group theory.
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A bijective ring homomorphisms is called a <term>ring
          isomorphism</term>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      Isomorphisms again carry the idea of two rings having exactly
      the same structure. If there is an isomorphism between two
      rings, then we say those two rings are <term>isomorphic</term>
      to each other and we treat them as the same ring with
      potentially different superficial presentation. 
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-ideals">
    <title>Ideals</title>
    <p>
      In group theory, normal subgroups were the kernels of
      homomorphisms, and quotients were formed only with normal
      subgroups. The same holds in ring theory, which leads to this
      definition. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>R</m> be a ring. A subset <m>I \subset R</m> is
          called an <term>ideal</term> if is it the kernel of some
          homorphism with domain <m>R</m>. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      For group theory, this was equivalent to a condition on the
      cosets of a normal subgroup. Here, similarly, there is a
      condition, though it is quite different from the group theory
      condition. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A subring <m>I \subset R</m> of a ring is an ideal if the
          additional property holds.
          <me>
            \forall r \in R \forall a \in I,  ra \in I 
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      The subring condition for multiplication is just closure: if
      <m>a,b, \in S</m> the <m>ab \in S</m>. The condition for an ideal
      is stronger here: only one of the element needs to be in the
      subgring. The other factor can be literally any other ring
      element but the product still needs to be in the ideal. It's
      a stronger kind of closure under multiplication: for any ideal
      element, any product with any other ring element remains in the
      ideal. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Of course, some examples can help to understand this new strange
      condition. 
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        I said at the start that the integers are the archtypical
        ring, and the same holds for ideals. I already said that
        <m>n\ZZ</m> is always a subring. I now claim that this is an
        ideal. Why? Well, take <m>7\ZZ</m>, which is the subring of
        multiples of <m>7</m>. An element of this subring is a
        multiple of <m>7</m>: call it <m>7k</m>. Then I can take any
        other integer <m>m</m>. If I multiply the two, I get <m>m(7k)
        = 7(km)</m><mdash/>this is still a multiple of <m>7</m>. This
        subring is closed under multiplication by any ring element. It
        is an ideal.
      </p>
      <p>
        I recommend using <m>n\ZZ</m> as your first mental model of an
        ideal. All multiples of an integer have exactly the right
        property: any other products will remain multiples. This
        example can give grounding to the new property. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Consider the ring <m>C(\RR)</m> of continuous functions with
        domain <m>\RR</m>. I can defined a subring <m>S(0) \subset
        C(\RR)</m> which consists of all functions which satisfy
        <m>f(0) = 0</m>. This is a subring: the sum, additive inverse,
        or product of these functions retails that property. This is
        also an idea. If <m>f(0) = 0</m> and <m>g(x)</m> is any other
        continuous function, I can calculate <m>f(0)g(0) = 0 g(0) = 0
        </m>, so the product function still satisfies. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Similarly, for any real number <m>a \in \RR</m>, I can define
        <m>S(a) = \{ f \in C(\RR) | f(a) = 0\}</m> to be the ideal of
        functions which evaluate to 0 at the special point <m>a</m>. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Let <m>S \subset \RR[x]</m> be the set of all real polynomials
        for which the constant term is zero. I claim this an ideal. It
        is a subring: if I add two polyomials without a constant term,
        the constant term in the sum is just <m>0 + 0 = 0</m>.
        Similarly, if I distribute all of the terms when multiplying
        two such polynomials, I can only make non-constant terms. But
        then even more. If <m>p(x)</m> has a zero constant term and I
        multiply it by any other polynomial <m>q(x)</m>, then every
        term in <m>p(x)</m> will have <m>x^r</m> for some <m>r \geq
        1</m>. In the distribution of the multiplication, all those
        terms involve one term from <m>p(x)</m>, so I can never create
        a non-zero constant term. This is also an ideal. 
      </p>
    </example>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-ring-quotients">
    <title>Ring Quotients</title>
    <p>
      I said that a ring ideal would be the equivalent to a normal
      subgroup. Let me now justify this claim.
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>I \subset R</m> be an ideal in a ring. Then the
          quotient <m>R/I</m> also has the structure of a ring under
          the induced two binary operations.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          I can make use of the group theory I already know. An
          ideal <m>I</m> is a subring, which means that it is a
          subgroup for the additive binary operation. Since the
          underlying additive group of a ring is abelian, all
          subgroups are normal. Therefore, <m>R/I</m> is already a
          well-defined quotient group. 
        </p>
        <p>
          The elements of the quotient group <m>R/I</m> are cosets,
          which can be written as <m>r + I</m> for <m>r \in R</m> some
          ring element. The multiplicative operation on the ring leads
          pretty naturally to the following operation on these cosets. 
          <me>
            (r + I)(s + I) = rs + I
          </me>
          To prove that the quotient is a ring, I need to prove that
          this is a well-defined associative binary operation and that
          it distributes over the addition. 
        </p>
        <p>
          The multiplication of the cosets is the multiplication
          <m>(r + i_1)(s + i_2)</m> for elements <m>i_1, i_2 \in
          I</m>. As these are all actual elements of the original
          ring, I can distribute everything. 
          <me>
            (r + i_i)(s + i_2) = rs + si_i + ri_2 + i_1i_2
          </me>
          I want this to be a the coset <m>(rs +
          I)</m>, but the distribution makes this tricky.
          <m>i_2i_2</m> is certainly in the idea, but what about
          <m>si_1</m> and <m>ri_2</m>? Well, this is where the
          definition of an ideal is important. In an ideal,
          multiplication by any other ring element remains in the
          idea. Therefore, <m>si_1</m> and <m>ri_2</m> are both in the
          ideal. This means that the sum <m>(si_2 + ri_2 + i_1i_2)</m>
          is in the ideal, so <m>rs + (si_2 + ri_2 +i_1i_2) \in rs
          + I</m>. This makes the multiplication work: it is
          well-defined. 
        </p>
        <p>
          Then I need to check distribution, but I actually get this
          almost for free from the known ring operations. Here is the
          calculation.
          <md>
            <mrow>
              (r + I) \left[ (s + I) + (t + I) \right] \amp = 
              (r + I) ((s + t) + I) = r(s + t) + I 
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = (rs + rt) + I = (rs + I) + (rt + I) 
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = (r + I)(s + I) + (r + I)(t + I)
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      So the quotient by an ideal works as a ring, and the proof shows
      that if I just tried to use any subring, the quotient would
      have failed in the multiplication step. The ideal is the
      equivalent to a normal subgroup. 
    </p>
    <p>
      As with group theory, there is an important and deep connection
      between quotients, homomorphisms and kernels. An ideal is the
      kernel of a homomorphism and produces a quotient; and any
      quotient leads to a homorphism.
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>I \subset R</m> be an idea of a ring. Then <m>\pi_I:
          R \rightarrow R/I</m> is the <term>projection
          homomorphism</term>. It sends any ring element <m>r \in
          R</m> to its coset <m>r + I</m>. By construction, the kernel
          of this homomorphism is exactly the  ideal <m>I</m>. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      Now I can state the equivalent versions to some of the important
      proporisions from group theory, kernels and quotients. 
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\phi: R \rightarrow S</m> be a ring homomorphism.
          Then <m>\phi</m> is injective if an only if <m>\Ker(\phi) =
          \{0\}</m>. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </proposition>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\phi: R \rightarrow S</m> be a surjective ring
          homomorphism. Then there exists a homomorphism. 
          <me>
            R / \Ker (\phi) \cong S 
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      Finally, let me end with some important types of ideals. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>r \in R</m> be the element of a ring. Then
          <m>rR = \{ rs | s \in R\}</m> is called <term>the ideal
          generated by <m>r</m></term>. Any idea generated by an
          element this way is called a <term>principal ideal</term>. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      A principal ideal generalizes the ideals <m>n\ZZ</m> that we
      have seen from the integers. It is infact true that all ideals
      of the integers have this form, but in other rings, there are
      ideals which are not principal. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>I \subset R</m> be an ideal in a ring. If any other
          ideal <m>J</m> such that <m>I \subset J \subset R</m> must
          be either <m>J = I</m> or <m>J = R</m>, then <m>I</m> is
          called a <term>maximal ideal</term>. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
  </subsection>
</section>
